There we go.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:29.763
All right, thank you very much.

00:01:29.763 --> 00:01:32.023
So I'm gonna talk about Bolivia indeed,

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.790
but I'm going to start by putting the data

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:40.333
and the specific research
in a bigger context

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:43.010
of comparative development
of Latin America

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.947
that I have worked on for many years

00:01:45.947 --> 00:01:47.390
and the "Return of Pachamama",

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:48.990
you'll see where Pachamama comes in.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:52.030
Pachamama is the earth mother,

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:55.530
kinda very powerful, important deity

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.390
for the Andean indigenous people.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:01.190
And this is a joint
project with Carlos Molina.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:05.600
Carlos is a PhD student in the
economics department in MIT,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.300
and Selaya Pablo is a professor at

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:09.510
the Economics Department at the

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:12.413
University of Copenhagen in Denmark.

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:18.380
Let me talk about the big picture

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.530
and then I'll get into the little picture.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:23.070
Here's a quote from one of the founders

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.033
of the MAS political party in Bolivia.

00:02:29.829 --> 00:02:33.300
He's talking about coca and
its relationship to Pachamama.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:38.300
So when you pray to
Pachamama, you offer coca

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:43.650
and other things and gifts
and sacrifices, okay?

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:46.070
So the way we're thinking
about this project is,

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:47.830
there's been an enormous
amount of research.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.180
I, myself have worked on this a lot,

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.650
kinda inspire with deep roots.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:53.491
I think the most obvious source

00:02:53.491 --> 00:02:57.930
of renewed excitement in this question,

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.830
this comparative
development of the Americas,

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:05.103
is this famous paper by
Angerman Sokolov in 1997.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:08.700
I must have read that paper 30 times.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.870
When I found it originally,
it blew my mind.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:15.110
It's a economic history, long run,

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.780
comparative development of the Americas,

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.810
emphasizing how colonialism
created these very different

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:25.300
societies in North America,
from South America.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:28.280
And that explains very different
patterns of inequality,

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.323
social mobility, long run development.

00:03:32.956 --> 00:03:35.930
Since they wrote there's being
some much more micro-work,

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:40.930
trying to dig into the nature
of Latin American institutions

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.690
and Latin American states and societies,

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:47.380
the sorts of things that
Angerman Sokolov talked about,

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.860
I think most famously Melissa Dell's

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.890
2010 paper on the Mit'a in Potosi,

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:57.010
where she examined in some
sense, the most famous

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:59.860
extractive institution
of the colonial period,

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:01.170
the Potosi, Mit'a.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:02.820
She looked at the catchment area,

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.260
the boundaries of the
catchment area of the Mit'a,

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:07.110
which has this huge labor draft

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.513
of the minds in Potosi in Bolivia.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.990
And she found that, if you compare places

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:16.410
just inside the Mit'a to
just outside the Mit'a,

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.970
places inside the Mit'a
had consumption levels,

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:22.810
which were a third less,
they had more child stunting

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:26.190
and she tells a brilliant story about how,

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:30.520
the Mit'a cast this long shadow over.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:34.020
You'll see by the end of my
talk, why I'm emphasizing this.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.570
Cast this long shadow
over economic development

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:40.500
in Peru and Bolivia.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.230
But I guess our starting point,

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.860
and this is something that
I've come to understand

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:47.780
from working a lot in Colombia,

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:51.793
and working a lot in
rural Colombia is that,

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:56.510
colonial states were
very patchwork things.

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:59.850
They controlled large
areas of Latin America

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:03.733
in a very, very uneven way.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:07.170
They were indirectly ruled by caciques,

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:10.800
by indigenous leaders in the
post-independence people,

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.400
by what's called Caudillos
in the historical literature.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.030
And even during the colonial period,

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:19.730
they are separated into,
the Republic for Spaniards

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:22.120
and Republica De Indios.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:25.570
So indigenous people were left separate,

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:26.667
self-governing and-

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:30.913
- Sorry to interrupt.

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:35.220
I'm worried that the slides
might be moving on your screen,

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:36.290
but not on the Zoom screen.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:39.640
We still have just the title slide on the-

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:40.750
- No, no, no.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:42.890
- Can you see anything now?

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:44.740
- So we can only see the title slide.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.950
I think your computer
might be treating as...

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:49.810
Oh, there.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:50.643
- Let me try again.

00:05:50.643 --> 00:05:52.350
Shall I try and share again?

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:53.183
- Sure.

00:05:53.183 --> 00:05:57.763
So the starting point for
this paper is, it's right.

00:06:00.633 --> 00:06:04.910
The story about extractive
institutions in Latin America,

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:07.950
exploitation of indigenous
people, creation of inequality.

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.737
But I want to say it's very uneven.

00:06:10.737 --> 00:06:12.510
And what we're trying to do in this paper,

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:16.010
I think is to dig into the
unevenness of the state

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.690
and the control and the span of the state,

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:22.450
and illustrate that this has
interesting possibilities.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:25.830
It's not all negative, it
has positive possibilities.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:28.320
And we think that's the story in Bolivia.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.220
I changed the slide, can you see that?

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:34.670
So large parts of colonial Latin America,

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:37.250
and post-colonial Latin America,

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.650
were organized, you could say,
in this Republica De Indios,

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:45.590
where many of the indigenous
institutions persisted.

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:49.220
Much of the cohesion of
indigenous society persisted.

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:52.903
And the word that we
like is, moral economy.

00:06:55.056 --> 00:06:57.070
This is a phrase that E.P. Thompson,

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:00.920
the British historian applied
to thinking about 18th century

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.413
British society, the moral economy,

00:07:04.296 --> 00:07:07.930
the set of rules and norms
and practices that were really

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:10.750
outside of the colonial state and outside

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:12.820
of the post-colonial state.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:16.497
And as James Scott argued
in his famous book,

00:07:16.497 --> 00:07:20.020
"The Moral Economy of The Peasant",

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:24.103
when these moral economies are
challenged, they can react.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:28.670
I'm gonna give one Colombian example.

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:30.620
Usually I talk far too
much about Colombia,

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:33.170
but one Colombian example,
which I always like,

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:35.940
just to illustrate this is,

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:40.040
Columbia is famous for being at one point,

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:43.663
the drug, the kidnapping, the
murder capital of the world.

00:07:45.386 --> 00:07:48.240
And in the story of Latin American

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:51.780
deep inequality and development divergence

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.510
that emanates Angerman Sokolov,

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:57.130
there's a lot of emphasis on
elites and I think rightly so.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:00.520
But I think what you
have to recognize is that

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:03.650
the hegemony of elites
has perhaps overemphasized

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:06.520
in a lot of this research,
including in my own research,

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:09.930
because in Columbia, it
wasn't Colombian elites

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:14.290
that wanted to turn the
country into the drug running,

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:17.340
homicide and kidnapping
capital of the world.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:19.110
They just couldn't stop it.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:20.210
Why couldn't they stop it?

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:23.480
Because they didn't control
large parts of Columbia.

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:26.423
There were large vacant spaces, vacuums,

00:08:27.335 --> 00:08:30.670
where other elites could emerge
and other political projects

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:35.490
could emerge and that's an
interesting and positive thing.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:36.560
At least we'll try to argue,

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:37.980
maybe not in the Colombian case.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:42.675
Now here's an example of
not being able to control

00:08:42.675 --> 00:08:46.960
half of Columbia, here's
three paramilitary warlords,

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:49.920
you could say, Ernesto Baez on the left,

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:51.850
Salvatore Mancuso in the middle,

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:54.890
and Ramon Isaza on the
right, what are they doing?

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:57.550
They're in Congress with suits on,

00:08:57.550 --> 00:08:59.380
negotiating with the politicians.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:02.240
So you don't control the periphery.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.520
You negotiate with it.

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:06.140
And that's a different image, I think,

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:11.140
from a lot of this work
emphasizing very hegemonic elites.

00:09:13.618 --> 00:09:16.100
So in this project, what we're going to do

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:19.470
is study what we think of as the violation

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:22.720
of the moral economy in rural Bolivia.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:27.720
So this moral economy, deeply
rooted in the pre-Andean past

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:31.530
reproducing itself through
the colonial period,

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:34.500
through the post-colonial
period, in some sense,

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:38.567
to quote C.L.R. James, his
book on the Haitian revolution,

00:09:38.567 --> 00:09:41.740
"It was a volcano waiting to go off!"

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:45.060
He uses this metaphor
that the French in Haiti,

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.830
were sleeping on the
edge of Vesuvius, okay?

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:50.810
It was sitting there, the
volcano was waiting to go off.

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:52.450
It didn't go off for hundreds of years,

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:54.330
but then it did go off.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:57.830
And I think that's the way
we want you to think about

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:01.120
what's happened in Bolivia
in the last 20 years.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:05.740
The volcano went off and
there's been a tectonic change

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:09.173
in the nature of politics and society.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:12.480
And so we're gonna look at
that Bolivian experience,

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:15.500
but I want you to think more broadly that,

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:18.320
perhaps we haven't
conceptualized properly,

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:20.880
the nature of the state in Latin America,

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:24.550
very uneven control of
much of its territory,

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:27.440
and the possibilities that perhaps that

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:31.450
allows for that we haven't
grasped, including possibilities,

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:35.220
dramatic change, which is
what we've seen in Bolivia.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:38.000
So what set off the volcano in Bolivia?

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.380
Well, our argument is it's basically,

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:43.740
the eradication of coca.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:47.740
Remember you have to use
coca to honor Pachamama.

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:51.000
In 1997, Hugo Banzer,
who was the president,

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:54.230
struck a deal with the
United States aiming at,

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:57.680
I say sometimes, or we
say sometimes, zero coca.

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:00.530
The reason I we say sometimes
is that it's a little uneven.

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:02.180
If you look at all the documents,

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:07.040
it's a little uneven, what was going on,

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:08.920
and different documents
say different things,

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:11.310
but the basic thrust of
it seems to have been

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:14.593
President Banzer agreed to eliminate coca.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:17.760
So what we're gonna look at in the data is

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:20.610
the consequence of this coca eradication

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:25.610
for politics in Bolivia
and the social consequences

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:30.000
that that has had, okay?

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.290
But I think to grasp,
why we're looking at coca

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:35.800
and the political consequences,

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:37.883
the attempt to eradicate coca,

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:42.640
if you're a Colombian, then
you say eradicate coca,

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:45.880
you think of helicopters
and planes spraying,

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:48.700
but this is how they do it
in Bolivia with a machete.

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:51.370
So just to give you the picture.

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:54.480
And here's just some sense,
the paper in the nutshell

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.200
as we're gonna get much
more into different things.

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:02.170
Here is the drug eradication
money on the left-hand axis,

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:05.140
flowing from the United
States that ramps up with this

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:10.060
Plan Dignidad, ironically
named the Plan Dignidad.

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:11.870
The bands are signs with
the U.S. government.

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:15.800
So there's a huge ramping
up of counter narcotics aid,

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.900
here's the voucher of
the MAS political party.

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:20.550
I'll talk a little bit about MAS.

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:24.370
Origins of MAS are very complicated.

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:26.820
It was organized by the cocaleros,

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:29.640
by these coca farmers
in the Chapare Valley,

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:32.890
in the east of Bolivia, but
there's many other groups

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:35.180
involved in it to start
with leftist groups,

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:36.930
women's groups, indigenous groups.

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:39.470
So let me not get into
the detailed history.

00:12:39.470 --> 00:12:43.463
You could say this United Left Party,

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:50.060
Evo Morales was involved with
was anticipated a little bit.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:54.540
The first time MAS really
runs as a party is in 1999.

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:57.083
And then you can see the
vote share rocket stop,

00:12:59.589 --> 00:13:02.610
and Morales wins the
presidential election in 2005.

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:08.360
Then of course, he abandons
the counter narcotics policy,

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:10.730
through aid with with us away.

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:12.690
So what we're gonna be
mostly interested in,

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:16.280
is this period here of the impact

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:18.100
of this counter narcotics program,

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:21.090
on political support from us.

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:25.200
And then what were the
consequences, which came...

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:27.730
Some of the consequences
as a result of this

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:31.123
capture of the state,
which happens in 2005.

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:38.530
Anyone who's been in
Bolivia, in rural Bolivia

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:42.163
will know this already,
but as an Englishman,

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:46.960
to give you an analogy, an English analogy

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:50.420
of what would it be like?

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:53.570
What's the analogy in England
of banning coca in Bolivia?

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:55.790
It would be like banning beer.

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:58.453
If you ban beer and
close down all the pubs,

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:02.620
what would English people do?

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:06.760
I guess that's a bit trivial, really,

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:10.530
because coca is just
much more deeply embedded

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:12.490
in the culture and the religious

00:14:12.490 --> 00:14:15.963
and the worldview of Andean people.

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:19.180
So here's Alan Kolata,
who's a colleague of mine

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:20.340
at the university of Chicago.

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:24.900
who's a very important scholar
of this part of the world,

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:29.610
and of indigenous society,
talking about the most important

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:33.210
institutionalized nation
of indigenous society

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:36.680
amongst the Aymara people in Bolivia.

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:38.840
So of course, Bolivia is very complicated.

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:41.443
It's very heterogeneous, ethnically.

00:14:42.903 --> 00:14:46.610
In the Andean area, the
largest number of people

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:49.120
are Aymara or Quechua.

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:54.120
So the Ayllu is the name for a
local local Aymara community.

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:58.530
So here he's just describing
what the Ayllu is.

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:00.603
Principles of common identity.

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:03.750
Let me move this thing so
I could read it for you.

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:05.070
Principles are common identity,

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:08.160
mobilized around and
deeply sedimented through

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:11.325
interpersonal intimacy
drive from shared history,

00:15:11.325 --> 00:15:13.530
shared labor, shared meals,

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:15.790
shared worship of a common ancestor.

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:19.793
So there's an enormous amount
of cooperative behavior,

00:15:20.710 --> 00:15:25.710
very communal, many very famous reciprocal

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:28.083
institutions of labor sharing,

00:15:28.925 --> 00:15:31.590
collective activity for
building houses, cooking,

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:33.550
providing public goods and in fact,

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:35.550
you have one of the most
famous scholars of this,

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:38.860
Enrique Mayer in the
Anthropology Department at Yale.

00:15:38.860 --> 00:15:40.070
So you're in a great place,

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:41.920
if you want to learn more about this.

00:15:43.897 --> 00:15:45.390
The Ayllu and the Marka

00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:47.910
is the analogous Quechua institution,

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:51.160
is the main way that indigenous
society is organized.

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:53.200
And I think most of the scholars of this

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:55.350
see this as very historical.

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:58.420
So this is part, you
could say of, this story

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:00.620
I was telling you the
Republican De Indios.

00:16:00.620 --> 00:16:03.010
This immense persistence of this

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:05.570
rural organization of indigenous people,

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:09.130
and many of the practices
and beliefs over time.

00:16:09.130 --> 00:16:10.930
And I want to say that,

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:13.860
this is very connected with coca.

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:15.770
So Catherine Allen,
who's an anthropologist

00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:17.610
at George Washington
University has written

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:21.490
a lot about this, about
how, it's one thing to say,

00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:23.160
there's labor reciprocity.

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:25.370
You help me, I help you,

00:16:25.370 --> 00:16:27.050
but I don't do anything without,

00:16:27.050 --> 00:16:29.150
and I don't ask anything from you without

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:31.363
offering coca with what they call kintu.

00:16:31.363 --> 00:16:33.210
I have to offer you three coca leaves.

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:35.730
If you worked in west
Africa, it's like cola,

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:37.050
cola and palm wine.

00:16:37.050 --> 00:16:39.370
If you want to do anything
or talk to someone

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:44.370
or meet someone, you break
cola and you have palm wine,

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:49.370
so here it's coca and
cane liquor nowadays,

00:16:49.370 --> 00:16:50.520
that's not very historic.

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:52.820
I guess, chicha or something
would have been more historic,

00:16:52.820 --> 00:16:57.590
but cane liquor is more
practical perhaps nowadays.

00:16:57.590 --> 00:16:59.343
And I won't go into this agenda.

00:17:01.198 --> 00:17:02.068
I'm just throwing it out there,

00:17:02.068 --> 00:17:03.853
I'm not gonna read it just to say,

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:06.850
coca is just cements.

00:17:06.850 --> 00:17:11.350
Everything is coca, people
exchanging coca, giving coca.

00:17:11.350 --> 00:17:15.313
It's just absolutely
central to people's lives.

00:17:16.458 --> 00:17:18.130
So I think when you understand that,

00:17:18.130 --> 00:17:22.750
you see how crazy this trying
to abolish coca and make coke,

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:26.773
not just eradicate coca,
but make coca illegal,

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:32.320
this is just an enormous
violation of this moral economy.

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:33.993
That's the argument here.

00:17:35.570 --> 00:17:38.200
Here's Pablo doing kintu,

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:40.150
when we were doing fieldwork last year.

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:45.100
This is the sort of thing
where, when you do field work,

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:47.143
this just really hits you,

00:17:48.925 --> 00:17:52.340
and it brings home how central
is says to people's lives

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:54.853
and the way they think about things.

00:17:55.979 --> 00:17:59.090
So I regret that that superficial analogy

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:00.270
to Englishmen drinking beer.

00:18:00.270 --> 00:18:03.610
This is much more serious
and important than that.

00:18:03.610 --> 00:18:04.443
Okay.

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:11.220
There's a very rich
secondary literature about

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:13.850
the rise of the MAS and the
organization of the MAS,

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:16.587
and the connection between
the MAS and the Ayllu

00:18:16.587 --> 00:18:19.240
and these indigenous organizations.

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:23.900
So there's very interesting
work by Bolivian scholars

00:18:23.900 --> 00:18:26.270
looking at that, so let
me not dwell on that.

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:27.933
But many leaders of the MAS,

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:31.960
many representatives of the
MAS in national institutions

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:33.828
or local institutions,

00:18:33.828 --> 00:18:37.090
they have roots in the
Ayllu and the Marka.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:40.330
They've served in executive positions.

00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:42.970
They have very interesting
political institutions,

00:18:42.970 --> 00:18:44.490
the Ayllu and the Marka.

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:46.710
They rotate every year.

00:18:46.710 --> 00:18:49.800
Everyone takes it in turn
to do different positions

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:52.860
within the hierarchy,
different executive positions.

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:57.810
And it's a very democratic,
republican type of thing,

00:18:57.810 --> 00:18:59.180
but I won't go into that.

00:18:59.180 --> 00:19:02.860
So just to say, it's,
well-documented, this connection

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:05.513
between these indigenous
institutions and the mass.

00:19:08.946 --> 00:19:11.360
Just to anticipate the
results and I'll talk about

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:13.603
what data we use to talk about this.

00:19:15.850 --> 00:19:17.220
What I'm gonna show you to start with.

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:18.140
Remember that picture,

00:19:18.140 --> 00:19:21.480
there's this ramping up
of counter narcotics aid,

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:24.260
and there's this ramping
up of the mass vote share.

00:19:24.260 --> 00:19:26.920
What I'm going to show
you to start with is that,

00:19:26.920 --> 00:19:29.780
there's a very close
connection between coca,

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:32.370
not actual coca, but coca suitability

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:33.620
and the MAS vote share.

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:37.460
So in places that are suitable for coca,

00:19:37.460 --> 00:19:39.960
I'll explain how we
measure that in a minute,

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:43.010
the MAS vote share goes up differentially,

00:19:43.010 --> 00:19:45.890
when Plan Dignidad comes into operation.

00:19:45.890 --> 00:19:48.970
So empirically, the way
to think about this is,

00:19:48.970 --> 00:19:50.710
we're gonna take Plan Dignidad

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:54.880
as a aggregate shock affecting Bolivia.

00:19:54.880 --> 00:19:57.900
And then we're gonna look
at how that interacts

00:19:57.900 --> 00:19:59.823
with the old coca suitability.

00:20:01.826 --> 00:20:03.930
Much more interesting is,

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:06.430
we have a very detailed information

00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:09.950
on where these Ayllus are in Bolivia.

00:20:09.950 --> 00:20:14.740
So more broadly where indigenous land is.

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:18.420
So indigenous people
have been able to claim

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:22.240
rights to their traditional
lands in Bolivia.

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:23.950
And so we have a map of that.

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:27.790
So we can actually look not
just a coca suitability,

00:20:27.790 --> 00:20:30.270
but something much more
interesting, which is,

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:33.970
do we see a differential
increase in the vote share

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:36.690
of the MAS when Plan Dignidad comes in,

00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:39.690
in places where there are the Ayllu.

00:20:39.690 --> 00:20:43.100
And the answer is yes, and
none of this is confounded

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:45.030
by the development effect.

00:20:45.030 --> 00:20:47.613
So you might think, oh,
everyone's growing coca.

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:53.300
When Plan Dignidad comes in,
not a big negative shock.

00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:56.370
So of course, there's a political reaction

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:58.523
to a negative economic shock.

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:02.430
None of what I'm saying
is confounded by that.

00:21:02.430 --> 00:21:03.817
So we can control for that.

00:21:03.817 --> 00:21:06.840
And the other thing, which is fascinating,

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:08.720
for someone who works a lot in Africa,

00:21:08.720 --> 00:21:13.308
is that this doesn't seem to
be about ethnicity either.

00:21:13.308 --> 00:21:16.490
The ethnic political geography of Bolivia

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:19.793
is extremely complicated
as I talked about earlier.

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:24.680
None of this is confounded by...

00:21:24.680 --> 00:21:27.640
It's not being driven
by ethnicity in any way.

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:30.603
I'll tell you how we
control for that, okay?

00:21:32.604 --> 00:21:37.604
So there's a significant
differential impact

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:41.040
of this Plan Dignidad
on the MAS vote share,

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:44.100
in places which are coca suitable,

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:47.580
and more interesting where the Ayllu is.

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:50.670
So we can look at that
in a panel data setting,

00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:52.500
but also because we have this map,

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:55.630
we can actually do or show
the regression discontinuity

00:21:55.630 --> 00:21:58.581
results that we can look at
the boundary of the Ayllu,

00:21:58.581 --> 00:22:00.710
and what I'm gonna show you is that

00:22:00.710 --> 00:22:03.350
there's large jumps in the MAS vote share.

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:06.710
When I look at just inside

00:22:06.710 --> 00:22:09.210
the Ayllu boundary and just outside.

00:22:09.210 --> 00:22:11.980
So we have a couple of
ways of identifying,

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:15.200
of trying to identify the
causal effect of this.

00:22:16.450 --> 00:22:18.410
And then what are the
consequences of that?

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:22.060
So that's just trying
to show there's this,

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:23.683
coca that violates this moral economy,

00:22:23.683 --> 00:22:27.393
and then there's a massive
organization society,

00:22:28.907 --> 00:22:31.890
which elites can't stop, they can't stop,

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:33.650
and they capture the state.

00:22:33.650 --> 00:22:35.810
What happens when they capture the state?

00:22:35.810 --> 00:22:38.870
And we're gonna look at two strategies.

00:22:38.870 --> 00:22:41.930
One is social mobility
of indigenous people

00:22:41.930 --> 00:22:44.860
using data from the Latinobarometro.

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:49.620
There's a significant increase
in actual and expected

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:52.500
rates of social mobility
for indigenous people,

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:54.290
when the MAS come to power.

00:22:54.290 --> 00:22:59.200
And I think most interesting
is we managed to collect

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:02.950
6 million birth records.

00:23:02.950 --> 00:23:04.940
So we actually have the names

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:06.587
that people give their children.

00:23:06.587 --> 00:23:10.210
And what we can show is that
there's an enormous increase.

00:23:10.210 --> 00:23:15.210
This is 75% increase in
the incidence of people

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:17.633
giving their children indigenous names.

00:23:19.071 --> 00:23:22.250
So we want to say, we call that
the empowerment effect that

00:23:22.250 --> 00:23:24.930
indigenous people, instead
of giving their children

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.580
Spanish names, and now are far more likely

00:23:27.580 --> 00:23:29.990
to be giving them indigenous names.

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:33.160
So towards that, reflects
the fact that this political

00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:38.130
change is really creating
a inclusive social change,

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:41.200
which I think anyone who goes to Bolivia,

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:44.210
it hits you in the face
if you're in El Alto

00:23:44.210 --> 00:23:46.880
or even in La Paz, spreading into La Paz.

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:49.883
So that, we're gonna document
that in a couple of ways.

00:23:53.154 --> 00:23:56.118
It's difficult to manage
questions and comments

00:23:56.118 --> 00:23:56.951
in a seminar like this.

00:23:56.951 --> 00:23:59.784
I don't know, Mushfiq, if you have

00:24:00.646 --> 00:24:01.833
what you think is optimal, I could stop,

00:24:01.833 --> 00:24:03.360
so that people ask questions,

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:06.970
or I could just plow ahead
and finish, before the end?

00:24:06.970 --> 00:24:09.420
- Actually, if there
are good places to pause

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:12.668
and give people 20 seconds,
like you're doing right now,

00:24:12.668 --> 00:24:15.700
that's great, but that
it's also not a regular

00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:17.070
econ seminar in the sense that

00:24:17.070 --> 00:24:19.290
there's people from all
different disciplines where

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:21.103
they're used to waiting till the end.

00:24:23.740 --> 00:24:25.640
As long as there no too
many questions right now,

00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:28.600
if you can wait until
the end, that's fine too.

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:30.690
- So I'm gonna talk a little
bit about the data now,

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:32.490
and I'll talk about...

00:24:32.490 --> 00:24:35.790
I'll try to leave at least
20 minutes at the end

00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:36.853
for people to ask questions.

00:24:36.853 --> 00:24:37.959
Is that good?

00:24:37.959 --> 00:24:39.470
Okay, all right.

00:24:39.470 --> 00:24:41.820
So let me just plow on
and talk about the data.

00:24:43.570 --> 00:24:47.170
To start the talk, the main
outcome variable is going to be

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:48.870
votes for the MAS, where we can either do

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:51.760
total number of votes or vote
share, it doesn't matter.

00:24:51.760 --> 00:24:55.020
And we have this data in
a very disaggregated form.

00:24:55.020 --> 00:24:57.110
So we have voting table data.

00:24:57.110 --> 00:25:00.870
So we have the data at the
individual voting tables.

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:03.350
We haven't been able to
get that for all elections,

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:07.370
but we've been able to get
it for these elections here.

00:25:07.370 --> 00:25:12.370
So 1999, 2004, 2005, 2009, 2010.

00:25:13.230 --> 00:25:14.580
In the regressions, I'm going to show you,

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:16.120
we're going to use total number of votes

00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:18.950
for the MAS at the voting table.

00:25:18.950 --> 00:25:20.710
So what does that look like?

00:25:20.710 --> 00:25:22.920
That's what all the voting
tables look like in Bolivia.

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:26.250
So it's very desegregated
and we're gonna situate

00:25:26.250 --> 00:25:28.540
the voting table in a pixel.

00:25:28.540 --> 00:25:32.810
That's 10 by 10 kilometer square,

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:36.180
and we're gonna measure
different things in that square,

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:40.467
such as, coca suitability,
such as presence of the Ayllu.

00:25:42.290 --> 00:25:45.020
I showed you the aggregate MAS vote share,

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:48.530
just to give you a sense of
how that changes over time,

00:25:48.530 --> 00:25:52.540
you can see, in 1999, when
they first really run as a...

00:25:52.540 --> 00:25:54.590
This is the Chapare Valley,

00:25:54.590 --> 00:25:56.680
where the cocaleros first got organized.

00:25:56.680 --> 00:26:00.320
So the MAS vote ramps up

00:26:00.320 --> 00:26:02.730
in this part of Cochabamba
in the department.

00:26:02.730 --> 00:26:05.080
And then, after that,

00:26:05.080 --> 00:26:09.030
this is when 2005, when Evo
wins the presidential election,

00:26:09.030 --> 00:26:12.690
you can see it's much more
widespread, but darker red means,

00:26:12.690 --> 00:26:15.470
had more vote share between 50 and 100%.

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:18.523
So this is very much in the Altiplano.

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:24.070
Coca suitability is using
data that people use a lot

00:26:24.070 --> 00:26:28.270
to measure different types of
crop suitability in economics.

00:26:28.270 --> 00:26:29.940
It's very popular nowadays.

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:33.283
The FAO have this database and for coca,

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:39.630
it gives what they call
optimal or absolute conditions,

00:26:41.450 --> 00:26:45.810
like for temperature, rainfall,
elevation slope, et cetera.

00:26:45.810 --> 00:26:48.780
So that gives you a interval.

00:26:48.780 --> 00:26:50.890
So for example, for temperature,

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:53.360
the optimal temperature
for growing coca would be

00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:57.923
18 to 20 degrees
centigrade, but absolutely,

00:26:59.344 --> 00:27:00.500
it's like a bigger window.

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:03.090
There's a bigger interval of temperatures.

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:06.850
So what we do is we just
take that data and we focus

00:27:06.850 --> 00:27:08.410
on the absolute, it doesn't matter.

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:13.410
So far, this seems to be robust
to doing it with optimal.

00:27:13.970 --> 00:27:16.340
And we just basically say,

00:27:16.340 --> 00:27:20.723
if you take a five kilometer
by five kilometer grid cell,

00:27:23.910 --> 00:27:25.470
if you're in the interval for temperature,

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:28.550
rainfall, elevation, slope,
et cetera, then that's a one.

00:27:28.550 --> 00:27:33.521
So that five by five kilometer grid cell

00:27:33.521 --> 00:27:35.930
is good for growing coca
and then we average it

00:27:35.930 --> 00:27:40.065
up to 10 by 10, which is what we're using.

00:27:40.065 --> 00:27:43.330
10 by 10 kilometer pixel, or grid cell

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:44.953
around the polling station.

00:27:45.820 --> 00:27:49.190
This is what it looks like
so the darker here is,

00:27:49.190 --> 00:27:52.620
the most suitable for growing coca.

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:55.750
Up again, tends to be not
completely in Altiplano,

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:57.550
it has to be down a little bit warmer,

00:27:57.550 --> 00:27:59.520
the Chapare or the Yungas is down.

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:04.420
So it's too cold up in
El Alto to grow coca.

00:28:04.420 --> 00:28:05.823
You have to be down a bit, but of course,

00:28:05.823 --> 00:28:09.200
this large area along the
Eastern side of the Sierra here

00:28:10.910 --> 00:28:13.840
which is highly suitable
for growing coca, okay?

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:16.060
So we have this stage
from the U.S. government.

00:28:16.060 --> 00:28:19.710
On the counter-narcotics
aid, that's very standard.

00:28:19.710 --> 00:28:22.640
I showed you what that looks
like just on that chart before.

00:28:22.640 --> 00:28:24.990
So I won't talk about
that in too much detail.

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:27.050
And we have this wonderful map.

00:28:27.050 --> 00:28:28.640
Here it is of...

00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:34.393
As I said, the Ayllu is
the Aymara word for this,

00:28:35.300 --> 00:28:38.920
but more broadly, communal land.

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:40.990
So there's a bit of slip.

00:28:40.990 --> 00:28:42.550
I'm being a little bit naughty here,

00:28:42.550 --> 00:28:45.320
but just to clarify
what we're going after,

00:28:45.320 --> 00:28:47.680
because for example, down
here, if you can see my cursor,

00:28:47.680 --> 00:28:49.480
this would be Guarani people.

00:28:49.480 --> 00:28:52.260
This is not Aymara or Quechua people,

00:28:52.260 --> 00:28:54.663
living down here on the
board with Paraguay.

00:28:56.305 --> 00:28:58.520
So what I'm gonna show you today is,

00:28:58.520 --> 00:29:01.120
we're gonna use all of
this, but you can see that.

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:04.650
So we can map this onto
this 10 by 10 grid cells.

00:29:04.650 --> 00:29:06.650
But we can also do this regression,

00:29:06.650 --> 00:29:08.920
discontinuity design, I guess,

00:29:08.920 --> 00:29:10.930
which probably is more convincing as a way

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:13.450
of trying to identify the causal effect

00:29:14.918 --> 00:29:16.668
of the impact of this Plan Dignidad

00:29:18.900 --> 00:29:23.103
on the vote share of the
MAS and we're gonna control,

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:29.010
I don't want to talk too
much about development.

00:29:29.010 --> 00:29:30.420
We want to say,

00:29:30.420 --> 00:29:33.130
this is not really a
story about developments.

00:29:33.130 --> 00:29:36.933
It's not that, you're growing coca,

00:29:40.360 --> 00:29:42.040
they eliminate coca crops.

00:29:42.040 --> 00:29:43.850
They come with a machete,
they cut it down.

00:29:43.850 --> 00:29:45.510
That's a huge negative shock.

00:29:45.510 --> 00:29:48.860
So of course, you vote
against the government.

00:29:48.860 --> 00:29:50.830
But what I'm gonna show you actually,

00:29:50.830 --> 00:29:53.380
and this is very nice in
the regression discontinuity

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:56.050
results is that, even in places

00:29:56.050 --> 00:29:58.070
which are completely unsuitable for coca,

00:29:58.070 --> 00:30:00.520
you have this very
significant treatment effect

00:30:00.520 --> 00:30:02.880
of being in the Ayllu.

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:05.630
So people who are clearly not involved

00:30:05.630 --> 00:30:09.330
up in the Altiplano, who are
clearly not growing coca,

00:30:09.330 --> 00:30:13.090
they also vote massively in favor of MAS

00:30:13.090 --> 00:30:15.040
when this plan comes in, why?

00:30:15.040 --> 00:30:18.893
Because this is just the
front to their society.

00:30:20.050 --> 00:30:23.240
So this is mostly trying to
control for developmental

00:30:23.240 --> 00:30:25.170
nightlight, it's just
trying to really show you,

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:27.340
that's not a confounder here because

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:30.370
if you know this literature,
then you might think,

00:30:30.370 --> 00:30:33.030
well, okay.

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:34.950
So and then there's the usual stuff,

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:37.670
latitude interacted with time dummies.

00:30:37.670 --> 00:30:39.750
So I'm not going to go into that.

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:40.910
Let me talk about that

00:30:40.910 --> 00:30:42.510
when I come to talk about social mobility.

00:30:42.510 --> 00:30:45.180
So the first part that the data is just

00:30:45.180 --> 00:30:48.793
using a very canonical panel data model.

00:30:50.467 --> 00:30:53.940
So this is why IT is going
to be total number of votes

00:30:53.940 --> 00:30:57.300
for MAS at polling station I in T,

00:30:57.300 --> 00:31:01.400
and T are these waves of
elections that we have,

00:31:01.400 --> 00:31:03.460
and then there's polling
station fixed effects

00:31:03.460 --> 00:31:07.740
as time effects and then
we have DT is this total,

00:31:07.740 --> 00:31:09.170
this is it's aggregate shock.

00:31:09.170 --> 00:31:12.930
It's this total amount of
us drug eradication aid.

00:31:12.930 --> 00:31:15.520
Aid in inverted commas,
I guess you could see.

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:19.530
CI is this index of coca suitability,

00:31:19.530 --> 00:31:23.350
in the pixel around a polling station I,

00:31:23.350 --> 00:31:27.220
and AI is gonna be in
these first regressions,

00:31:27.220 --> 00:31:30.670
the proportion of land in
that pixel, which is held by

00:31:30.670 --> 00:31:32.943
the Ayllu or indigenous communities.

00:31:34.330 --> 00:31:37.860
So we want to see if there's
differential effects,

00:31:37.860 --> 00:31:41.323
and then we can also look at
the triple interaction here.

00:31:45.710 --> 00:31:48.900
So here's the results which
are most interesting, I think.

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:52.350
So let me jump to the results
that are most interesting.

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:53.893
So what do we find here?

00:31:55.160 --> 00:31:58.950
We find a significant positive
effect of coca suitability

00:31:58.950 --> 00:32:03.883
on number of votes for
MAS on the left hand side.

00:32:04.990 --> 00:32:07.450
So we find that very robust.

00:32:07.450 --> 00:32:09.750
Down here, there's all sorts
of different co-variants

00:32:09.750 --> 00:32:12.900
we can play with and I guess
what I want to say here,

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:16.020
is that when you control
for nighttime lights,

00:32:16.020 --> 00:32:19.049
I mean, you could say this
would be a bad control

00:32:19.049 --> 00:32:21.470
in the Angrist and Pesky sense.

00:32:21.470 --> 00:32:22.990
But anyway, I think in this context,

00:32:22.990 --> 00:32:25.250
it's interesting to look at
whether that's the channel,

00:32:25.250 --> 00:32:28.560
and that does nothing to the coefficients

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:30.170
that we're interested in.

00:32:30.170 --> 00:32:32.050
You see this positive significance effect

00:32:32.050 --> 00:32:35.230
for coca suitability and
also for community land

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:37.240
and the triple...

00:32:37.240 --> 00:32:40.650
So just to emphasize here,

00:32:40.650 --> 00:32:43.530
these coefficients are the
coefficients on the interaction

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:48.040
between this aggregate U.S drug aid,

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:52.910
and coca suitability, and
the proportion of Ayllu

00:32:52.910 --> 00:32:55.250
community land within that grid cell.

00:32:55.250 --> 00:32:57.170
So that's like a double interaction.

00:32:57.170 --> 00:32:59.583
So this is like the
triple interaction, but,

00:33:00.440 --> 00:33:03.363
we never find that to be significant.

00:33:04.700 --> 00:33:08.040
So yeah, coca suitability matters.

00:33:08.040 --> 00:33:10.200
Why does coca suitability matter?

00:33:10.200 --> 00:33:12.610
I think the way we're thinking about that

00:33:12.610 --> 00:33:15.823
is that that's picking
up in some sense that,

00:33:17.943 --> 00:33:21.137
the location of this coca culture.

00:33:24.087 --> 00:33:26.160
But of course the presence of the Ayllu

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:29.450
or community land for us
is much more interesting

00:33:29.450 --> 00:33:31.040
'cause that's really showing,

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:34.720
where this institutionalized
indigenous society is.

00:33:34.720 --> 00:33:37.890
Irrespective of coca suitability,

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:41.600
you find this positive,
significant effect on

00:33:41.600 --> 00:33:46.600
the MAS vote share when Plan
Dignidad comes into operation.

00:33:47.410 --> 00:33:50.640
And if you look at the
quantitative effects of this,

00:33:50.640 --> 00:33:54.680
you see that, one standard like so.

00:33:54.680 --> 00:33:57.163
Just to think about what
the Plan Dignidad did,

00:33:58.320 --> 00:34:02.560
it increased U.S counter
drug money by about 2.3

00:34:02.560 --> 00:34:05.380
standard deviations at its maximum.

00:34:05.380 --> 00:34:07.980
And what we find for example
with the Ayllu is that,

00:34:11.630 --> 00:34:15.890
when that comes in, a
one standard deviation

00:34:15.890 --> 00:34:20.270
in U.S counter-drug aid
leads to a 10% increase

00:34:20.270 --> 00:34:21.820
in the number of votes for MAS.

00:34:24.930 --> 00:34:26.980
So this is quantitatively, very large.

00:34:26.980 --> 00:34:27.930
That's what I want to show.

00:34:27.930 --> 00:34:31.800
So given the increase
in U.S counter-drug aid,

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:35.930
the net effect of these two
forces here, i's very big.

00:34:35.930 --> 00:34:38.060
Quantitatively, very big.

00:34:38.060 --> 00:34:38.893
Yes.

00:34:38.893 --> 00:34:40.227
- Jim, Orazio here.

00:34:40.227 --> 00:34:42.620
I have a clarification question.

00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:44.830
In your regression, do you have the...

00:34:44.830 --> 00:34:46.440
'Cause you didn't show them in the table,

00:34:46.440 --> 00:34:51.440
the direct effect of
coca stability and Ayllu,

00:34:54.582 --> 00:34:57.143
without the interaction
with the Plan Dignidad?

00:34:58.980 --> 00:35:01.413
- That's absorbed in the
fixed effects, isn't it?

00:35:03.240 --> 00:35:04.700
Time-invariant, right?

00:35:04.700 --> 00:35:05.600
- Okay.

00:35:05.600 --> 00:35:08.410
- Yeah, so it's absorbed
in the fixed effect.

00:35:08.410 --> 00:35:10.860
And I want to say, this
is not a simple story

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:12.710
of the rise of ethnic politics either.

00:35:12.710 --> 00:35:15.753
So what we do is we
took the ethnologue map,

00:35:16.680 --> 00:35:19.530
which has basically very
detailed information,

00:35:19.530 --> 00:35:23.490
on the presence of different
ethnolinguistic groups,

00:35:23.490 --> 00:35:26.440
really coded from language,
that's my understanding.

00:35:26.440 --> 00:35:30.150
But we can map that onto
the 10 by 10 grid cell.

00:35:30.150 --> 00:35:33.290
And so we can control for
the presence of different

00:35:33.290 --> 00:35:37.540
ethnicities, Aymara,
Quechua, Guarani or whatever.

00:35:37.540 --> 00:35:39.060
Again, that's the time invariant thing,

00:35:39.060 --> 00:35:41.463
but interactive with time dummies.

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:48.560
This is not confounded by
the rise of Aymara politics

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:51.370
or the rise of Quechua politics.

00:35:51.370 --> 00:35:53.630
And in fact, these institutions really

00:35:53.630 --> 00:35:55.640
go across ethnic groups.

00:35:55.640 --> 00:35:59.850
Especially Quechua and
Aymara, in a fascinating way.

00:35:59.850 --> 00:36:02.580
So I could get into that in
more detail, but I think that's

00:36:02.580 --> 00:36:06.123
probably not emphasized
enough at the moment.

00:36:08.810 --> 00:36:12.250
You might think, oh,
it's indigenous people

00:36:12.250 --> 00:36:14.480
have finally got mobilized,

00:36:14.480 --> 00:36:16.810
so this is the rise of
ethnic politics or something,

00:36:16.810 --> 00:36:18.570
but that's not the story we want to say.

00:36:18.570 --> 00:36:21.340
We want to say this is
about this violation

00:36:21.340 --> 00:36:22.693
of this moral economy.

00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:27.260
All right, so now we can do this.

00:36:27.260 --> 00:36:29.430
So that's the very straightforward

00:36:29.430 --> 00:36:31.090
panel data approach to this.

00:36:31.090 --> 00:36:33.330
We could do something a
little bit more sophisticated

00:36:33.330 --> 00:36:36.780
since we have the map,
which is, we can narrow in

00:36:36.780 --> 00:36:39.070
on the boundaries of these Ayllu

00:36:39.070 --> 00:36:42.390
and try to compare places
just inside the Ayllu,

00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:44.193
to just outside the Ayllu.

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:49.800
The analogous econometric model,

00:36:49.800 --> 00:36:52.180
that seems closest to what we estimated

00:36:52.180 --> 00:36:54.917
in the panel data model, is as follows.

00:36:54.917 --> 00:36:59.917
So think of AI, as just being the distance

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:02.310
of the particular polling.

00:37:02.310 --> 00:37:04.800
So now, again, we're thinking
about the polling stations,

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:07.120
because why YIT is, again, gonna be

00:37:07.120 --> 00:37:08.830
the votes of the MAS party,

00:37:08.830 --> 00:37:11.890
So we're gonna AI, like
the running variable,

00:37:11.890 --> 00:37:14.440
is the distance from the polling station

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:16.620
to the boundary, if you like.

00:37:16.620 --> 00:37:20.560
And then this is a very similar expression

00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:23.000
to what we had before,
but just introducing

00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:27.380
this regression discontinuity type model.

00:37:27.380 --> 00:37:30.530
But think of the variables, the same DT

00:37:30.530 --> 00:37:34.390
as the aggregate counter-drug aids,

00:37:34.390 --> 00:37:38.187
CI is your coca suitability
and AI is the Ayllu.

00:37:42.790 --> 00:37:45.470
Coca suitability goes
into the background here.

00:37:45.470 --> 00:37:48.520
We're really focusing on
that map and thinking about,

00:37:48.520 --> 00:37:51.490
what can we say at the
boundary of the Ayllu?

00:37:51.490 --> 00:37:54.520
Do we see significant
jumps in the vote share

00:37:54.520 --> 00:37:58.080
for MAS at the boundary?

00:37:58.080 --> 00:37:59.440
And the answer to that is yes.

00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:00.560
So here's a nice picture.

00:38:00.560 --> 00:38:04.450
So imagine I narrow into two places

00:38:04.450 --> 00:38:06.170
that are not coca suitable.

00:38:06.170 --> 00:38:07.640
So forget about coca.

00:38:07.640 --> 00:38:08.840
Well, don't forget about Coca,

00:38:08.840 --> 00:38:11.263
but forget about growing coca.

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:15.270
And here's the boundary, and
this is the MAS vote share

00:38:15.270 --> 00:38:18.480
when I go from being outside the Ayllu

00:38:18.480 --> 00:38:20.090
to being inside the Ayllu.

00:38:20.090 --> 00:38:23.903
So what we find is a significant
jump in the MAS vote share,

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:29.970
if the polling station
is inside the Ayllu.

00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:34.010
So what's nice about these
results, if I show you the table,

00:38:34.010 --> 00:38:37.520
is that the estimated
coefficients are extremely similar

00:38:38.920 --> 00:38:41.870
to the basic panel data regressions.

00:38:41.870 --> 00:38:43.570
We tried to do this in
a way which makes it

00:38:43.570 --> 00:38:46.207
as comparable as possible,

00:38:46.207 --> 00:38:49.260
'cause there's a lot of
robustness exercises here,

00:38:49.260 --> 00:38:52.903
in terms of how you specify
the running variable.

00:38:57.163 --> 00:38:58.270
This is the full sample.

00:38:58.270 --> 00:39:00.290
This is what I showed you before.

00:39:00.290 --> 00:39:02.950
Obviously you lose a
lot of the observations.

00:39:02.950 --> 00:39:07.850
Now we're zooming in on
the boundaries of the map.

00:39:07.850 --> 00:39:10.200
The boundaries of the Ayllu,
comparing polling stations

00:39:10.200 --> 00:39:12.230
just inside, just
outside, we're gonna lose

00:39:12.230 --> 00:39:15.450
a lot of polling stations
that are not close

00:39:15.450 --> 00:39:17.770
or not anywhere near to these boundaries,

00:39:17.770 --> 00:39:20.310
but the pattern here is very similar.

00:39:20.310 --> 00:39:22.570
We don't find the triple
interaction to be...

00:39:22.570 --> 00:39:24.690
I don't know why we even
have that there, really.

00:39:24.690 --> 00:39:27.370
We thought it was
interesting at some point.

00:39:27.370 --> 00:39:29.520
There's all this path
dependence in papers.

00:39:30.700 --> 00:39:33.160
So here's the thing I
want to really emphasize,

00:39:33.160 --> 00:39:35.680
which is this interaction between

00:39:35.680 --> 00:39:37.967
the counter-narcotics and the Ayllu.

00:39:39.970 --> 00:39:42.170
So that pattern is very similar.

00:39:42.170 --> 00:39:43.530
These results are very similar.

00:39:43.530 --> 00:39:45.410
They're just dropping
a lot of observations,

00:39:45.410 --> 00:39:48.153
but they're zooming in
on, places where we think,

00:39:49.508 --> 00:39:52.550
there's more identification
and we find something

00:39:52.550 --> 00:39:55.960
very similar and it's very
robust to messing about with

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:58.280
different specifications
of the running variable,

00:39:58.280 --> 00:40:00.920
different bandwidths and things like that,

00:40:00.920 --> 00:40:03.560
With all the usual trade-offs.

00:40:04.720 --> 00:40:07.260
So let me talk a little bit
about the consequences then,

00:40:07.260 --> 00:40:08.360
and then I'll shut up.

00:40:10.030 --> 00:40:14.300
We look at two things and we
could look at more things,

00:40:14.300 --> 00:40:16.420
and I think the second
thing is more interesting.

00:40:16.420 --> 00:40:18.140
So let me go first over this.

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:21.010
The first thing we look at
is just social mobility.

00:40:21.010 --> 00:40:22.240
So how do we look at that?

00:40:22.240 --> 00:40:26.610
Well, Latinobarometro
asks questions about...

00:40:26.610 --> 00:40:31.093
It asks questions about where
you are on an income scale.

00:40:32.088 --> 00:40:35.010
So it asks you to put
yourself on an income scale

00:40:35.010 --> 00:40:37.830
between one to 10, it's
on an earlier slide.

00:40:37.830 --> 00:40:40.620
I think it's one to 10,
it could be zero to 10.

00:40:40.620 --> 00:40:43.380
It asks you to put your
parent on that scale,

00:40:43.380 --> 00:40:46.260
and it asks you to put your
child on that scale too.

00:40:46.260 --> 00:40:50.170
So it's anticipations
of the social mobility

00:40:50.170 --> 00:40:51.200
of your children.

00:40:51.200 --> 00:40:55.500
We could look at, do you
expect your children to be

00:40:55.500 --> 00:40:58.403
higher up this income scale than you are?

00:40:59.810 --> 00:41:01.740
And so, it's Latinobarometro,

00:41:01.740 --> 00:41:05.420
so it's not a panel, it's
a repeated cross section.

00:41:05.420 --> 00:41:10.420
So we're interested in,
this individual I's response

00:41:11.420 --> 00:41:15.620
to this income question in municipality.

00:41:15.620 --> 00:41:19.190
M at time T, we don't have
individual fixed effects

00:41:19.190 --> 00:41:20.023
'cause it's not a panel,

00:41:20.023 --> 00:41:22.700
but we can put municipality
fixed effects in,

00:41:22.700 --> 00:41:27.700
and we can look again at
aggregate counter-narcotics aids.

00:41:27.710 --> 00:41:29.490
And I'm sorry,

00:41:29.490 --> 00:41:30.660
that's not what we're gonna do at all.

00:41:30.660 --> 00:41:32.670
We're going to look at...

00:41:32.670 --> 00:41:34.340
We should chase a notation there.

00:41:34.340 --> 00:41:37.360
DT is not counter-narcotics
aid, forget that.

00:41:37.360 --> 00:41:39.490
We're looking at the consequences now.

00:41:39.490 --> 00:41:41.230
So DT is a dummy,

00:41:41.230 --> 00:41:45.760
which just switches on
when Evo wins the 2005

00:41:45.760 --> 00:41:47.400
presidential election.

00:41:47.400 --> 00:41:50.390
So now we're trying to
look at the consequences

00:41:50.390 --> 00:41:52.580
of this for indigenous people.

00:41:52.580 --> 00:41:57.450
So capital I, IMT is
self-reported ethnicity

00:41:57.450 --> 00:41:59.500
in the Latinobarometro,

00:41:59.500 --> 00:42:01.600
so that's the dummy one, if the person

00:42:01.600 --> 00:42:03.380
reports being indigenous.

00:42:03.380 --> 00:42:07.130
So we're interested in whether,
when Evo comes to power,

00:42:07.130 --> 00:42:11.460
you see differential social
mobility of indigenous people.

00:42:11.460 --> 00:42:12.980
And what's the answer?

00:42:12.980 --> 00:42:16.160
Well, here's the answer for you.

00:42:16.160 --> 00:42:18.703
This is your own reported income.

00:42:19.670 --> 00:42:21.820
So when Evo comes to power,

00:42:21.820 --> 00:42:23.990
comparing before and after what you see

00:42:23.990 --> 00:42:27.940
and indigenous and non-indigenous people,

00:42:27.940 --> 00:42:31.150
you see a significant increase

00:42:31.150 --> 00:42:35.223
in people's reported income.

00:42:37.140 --> 00:42:39.540
I don't know if there's a social mobility,

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:42.630
but this is an expected social mobility.

00:42:42.630 --> 00:42:46.520
That's the significant, and
much, much larger quantitatively

00:42:47.690 --> 00:42:50.110
increase in your expectations,

00:42:50.110 --> 00:42:52.930
the expectations of
indigenous people about

00:42:52.930 --> 00:42:55.623
where their children are
gonna be on this scale.

00:42:56.570 --> 00:42:59.210
And there's an interesting
placebo here you can do,

00:42:59.210 --> 00:43:01.577
which is we have
questions on your parents.

00:43:01.577 --> 00:43:04.890
So there's no way that
Evo coming into power

00:43:04.890 --> 00:43:07.010
could affect where your parents were,

00:43:07.010 --> 00:43:09.000
most likely seems plausible.

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:10.600
And then we don't find anything on that.

00:43:10.600 --> 00:43:13.420
So that's nice because
it's like a placebo,

00:43:13.420 --> 00:43:15.520
it makes the other
results more believable.

00:43:15.520 --> 00:43:17.780
It's not that everything is moving.

00:43:17.780 --> 00:43:21.810
I'm euphoric, so I'm
overestimating everything.

00:43:21.810 --> 00:43:25.050
No, nothing happens with parents, but,

00:43:25.050 --> 00:43:27.420
and I guess this is what we
think is most interesting.

00:43:27.420 --> 00:43:31.690
Then you see increased
expectations of indigenous people

00:43:31.690 --> 00:43:34.330
when Evo comes to power
of social mobility,

00:43:34.330 --> 00:43:36.500
measured in this way.

00:43:36.500 --> 00:43:40.543
Then the final exercise
before I shut up is to say,

00:43:41.400 --> 00:43:44.160
and remind you what the big picture is,

00:43:44.160 --> 00:43:49.160
so we have 6 million records of the names

00:43:49.810 --> 00:43:51.830
that people gave to their children.

00:43:51.830 --> 00:43:56.830
So what we're not able to do
is link parents to children.

00:43:57.820 --> 00:44:02.560
So in some sense, the ideal
regression we'd like to run is,

00:44:02.560 --> 00:44:05.750
if I took the name of
a child and we could...

00:44:05.750 --> 00:44:07.570
What we did was we classified

00:44:07.570 --> 00:44:10.410
using a standard list in Bolivia.

00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:13.963
Was this an indigenous name
or was it a Spanish name?

00:44:14.850 --> 00:44:18.100
And we'd like to capture,
we'd like to measure,

00:44:18.100 --> 00:44:21.463
was child I in municipality in time T,

00:44:22.750 --> 00:44:25.240
did that child get an indigenous name?

00:44:25.240 --> 00:44:28.740
And we'd like to know whether
their family was indigenous,

00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:31.440
and then we'd like to look
at the interaction between.

00:44:32.290 --> 00:44:34.790
But we can't do that
because we can't link.

00:44:34.790 --> 00:44:36.580
So we don't know the identity.

00:44:36.580 --> 00:44:38.350
We have these records of naming,

00:44:38.350 --> 00:44:40.273
but we don't have the identity,

00:44:41.222 --> 00:44:42.703
or the name of the parent. Okay?

00:44:42.703 --> 00:44:45.430
So what we have to do is
something much less nice,

00:44:45.430 --> 00:44:48.810
which is, we do have the
names of all these children.

00:44:48.810 --> 00:44:50.720
So what we're gonna do is we average them

00:44:50.720 --> 00:44:52.543
at the level of the municipality.

00:44:53.750 --> 00:44:57.430
We don't know who their parents were,

00:44:57.430 --> 00:45:00.510
but from the census, we
can recover estimates

00:45:00.510 --> 00:45:03.483
of number of indigenous
people at the municipality.

00:45:05.125 --> 00:45:07.170
So this is coming from the census.

00:45:07.170 --> 00:45:09.440
So the left-hand side is coming from this

00:45:09.440 --> 00:45:12.420
6 million observations
of children's names.

00:45:12.420 --> 00:45:14.340
The right-hand side is
coming from the census,

00:45:14.340 --> 00:45:17.400
but it's at the municipality
level, unfortunately,

00:45:17.400 --> 00:45:19.990
and then we can interact
that with this dummy

00:45:19.990 --> 00:45:21.870
of Evo coming to power, we can control

00:45:21.870 --> 00:45:25.010
the time effects and
municipality fixed effects.

00:45:25.010 --> 00:45:27.060
And here's a picture, which is very nice.

00:45:28.210 --> 00:45:31.190
So you can see there's no pre trends here.

00:45:31.190 --> 00:45:34.320
When Evo comes to power in 2005,

00:45:34.320 --> 00:45:38.927
what you see is a systematic
increase in people.

00:45:42.620 --> 00:45:45.150
Subject to this, we don't
really have the micro data

00:45:45.150 --> 00:45:47.970
that we'd like, but
subject to that caveat,

00:45:47.970 --> 00:45:50.650
there seems to be something going on here

00:45:50.650 --> 00:45:55.300
in terms of a much larger
proportion of people,

00:45:55.300 --> 00:45:58.320
indigenous people start
naming their children

00:45:58.320 --> 00:46:00.713
with indigenous names and as I said,

00:46:01.610 --> 00:46:05.643
that's what the regression
looks like and quantitatively.

00:46:06.760 --> 00:46:08.820
We don't have 6 million
observations here because

00:46:08.820 --> 00:46:13.500
we're operating at this
municipality level, unfortunately.

00:46:13.500 --> 00:46:14.653
So we had to average.

00:46:16.670 --> 00:46:19.230
And quantitatively it's
very large, as I said,

00:46:19.230 --> 00:46:21.580
so we can do this as just
looking at the fraction

00:46:21.580 --> 00:46:23.370
and this is the total number of children

00:46:23.370 --> 00:46:24.820
with indigenous names

00:46:24.820 --> 00:46:28.033
and quantitatively the
effect is very large.

00:46:30.146 --> 00:46:32.920
The treatment effect is
about 75% of the mean

00:46:32.920 --> 00:46:36.530
in terms of the fraction
of children that have

00:46:36.530 --> 00:46:38.233
indigenous names to start with.

00:46:41.600 --> 00:46:43.500
There's more stuff here, but let me...

00:46:44.830 --> 00:46:47.910
This is just like placebos
and just thinking about it.

00:46:47.910 --> 00:46:50.200
Let me try to wrap up here.

00:46:50.200 --> 00:46:52.470
So when I started, I talked about,

00:46:52.470 --> 00:46:55.670
and said why I think this is interesting,

00:46:55.670 --> 00:46:58.900
there's this hegemonic narrative

00:46:58.900 --> 00:47:03.900
of Latin American developments
or comparative development

00:47:04.560 --> 00:47:08.360
within the Americas and
I think it's not wrong.

00:47:08.360 --> 00:47:11.170
I think Melissa's results for example,

00:47:11.170 --> 00:47:13.200
I believe the results,

00:47:13.200 --> 00:47:16.460
but I think what this experience
in Bolivia shows is that,

00:47:16.460 --> 00:47:19.460
this equilibrium is
actually a lot more fragile

00:47:19.460 --> 00:47:22.220
than it appears when you
read this literature,

00:47:22.220 --> 00:47:25.260
that once you start thinking
and understanding a bit about

00:47:25.260 --> 00:47:28.720
the kind of architecture of
the state in Latin America,

00:47:28.720 --> 00:47:32.590
large parts of Bolivia,
were not really controlled

00:47:32.590 --> 00:47:34.780
by Personio elites.

00:47:34.780 --> 00:47:37.670
They were controlled by
indigenous institutions

00:47:37.670 --> 00:47:40.410
and indigenous society by
the Ayllu and the Marka

00:47:40.410 --> 00:47:43.890
and with very little hegemonic control

00:47:45.062 --> 00:47:46.337
from LaPaz or anywhere else.

00:47:46.337 --> 00:47:48.360
And that's true in Columbia, it's true

00:47:48.360 --> 00:47:51.530
in all sorts of parts of Latin America,

00:47:51.530 --> 00:47:54.930
so that equilibrium can be perturbed

00:47:56.189 --> 00:47:58.970
in very interesting ways.

00:47:58.970 --> 00:48:02.540
And that's exactly what
happened in this one instance.

00:48:02.540 --> 00:48:06.090
We're looking at a very
specific perturbation

00:48:06.090 --> 00:48:09.078
in that equilibrium, a kind of policy that

00:48:09.078 --> 00:48:12.900
enormously disrupted the
worldview or the values,

00:48:12.900 --> 00:48:14.710
if you like, of indigenous society

00:48:14.710 --> 00:48:17.580
and led to this very
powerful, political reaction

00:48:17.580 --> 00:48:22.580
that captured the state and
led to good things, I think.

00:48:22.830 --> 00:48:25.370
More inclusive things,
indigenous empowerment,

00:48:25.370 --> 00:48:27.160
empowerment of women, also,

00:48:27.160 --> 00:48:29.130
we don't really have results on that here,

00:48:29.130 --> 00:48:31.240
but if you've been in Bolivia,

00:48:31.240 --> 00:48:35.680
women are very empowered in
interesting ways in Bolivia,

00:48:35.680 --> 00:48:40.210
in traditional society
and today in the markets

00:48:40.210 --> 00:48:41.883
in El Alto and everywhere else.

00:48:42.811 --> 00:48:46.310
So I think the reason I
like this paper is that

00:48:49.533 --> 00:48:51.510
there's a silver lining to the clouds,

00:48:51.510 --> 00:48:53.700
to the Latin American clouds, it seems.

00:48:53.700 --> 00:48:57.430
And where there's one silver
lining, there may be more.

00:48:57.430 --> 00:49:00.630
Just to finish by talking
about Melissa's paper again,

00:49:00.630 --> 00:49:03.760
the ironic thing is that a
lot of this action is actually

00:49:03.760 --> 00:49:06.900
happening in exactly
these Mit'a areas where

00:49:06.900 --> 00:49:09.790
she studied, and in
fact, Francisco Toledo,

00:49:09.790 --> 00:49:13.100
who was the man who laid out the Mit'a,

00:49:13.100 --> 00:49:14.870
who was the viceroy who laid out the Mit'a

00:49:14.870 --> 00:49:18.000
and set up that catchment area.

00:49:18.000 --> 00:49:19.480
One thing he did was to abolish

00:49:19.480 --> 00:49:21.473
all restrictions on coca growing,

00:49:23.153 --> 00:49:25.280
so there's an interesting
connection to the Mit'a

00:49:25.280 --> 00:49:27.033
and Francisco Toledo.

00:49:28.124 --> 00:49:29.870
So let me shut up now,

00:49:29.870 --> 00:49:32.710
I'm happy to answer questions
about the econometrics

00:49:32.710 --> 00:49:34.030
if people want to go back to that

00:49:34.030 --> 00:49:37.003
or answer questions about anything.

00:49:38.160 --> 00:49:38.993
- Thanks very much, Jim.

00:49:38.993 --> 00:49:42.360
So we do have some
time, so just open it up

00:49:42.360 --> 00:49:44.840
and maybe you could use
the hand raise feature

00:49:44.840 --> 00:49:47.420
if you'd like, or just
unmute yourself and speak.

00:49:47.420 --> 00:49:48.670
And if there's multiple questions,

00:49:48.670 --> 00:49:50.970
then we'll start using
the hand raise feature.

00:49:52.860 --> 00:49:54.120
- [Sanjeeev] I have a question.

00:49:54.120 --> 00:49:58.443
So do we know what was the
source of increase in income?

00:50:00.330 --> 00:50:02.950
It seems like in these areas
were also doing better.

00:50:02.950 --> 00:50:06.170
Was it because of nationalization
of mining that happened

00:50:06.170 --> 00:50:11.170
in Bolivia and it seems, do
we know the reason for that?

00:50:11.813 --> 00:50:15.020
Was it a transfer from
the government to the...

00:50:20.090 --> 00:50:22.150
- Yeah, the survey doesn't reveal that.

00:50:22.150 --> 00:50:26.500
The survey just asks, it
asks you to place yourself,

00:50:26.500 --> 00:50:30.360
for example, on this scale between zero...

00:50:32.530 --> 00:50:35.230
I think there are other
questions about occupations.

00:50:35.230 --> 00:50:40.230
So not the anticipated
occupation of your children,

00:50:40.600 --> 00:50:44.653
but it might be, we might
be able to look at that.

00:50:46.210 --> 00:50:48.230
The data that we have, or we've been using

00:50:48.230 --> 00:50:50.163
doesn't directly speak to that.

00:50:54.440 --> 00:50:59.300
The reason that I like
these naming regressions,

00:50:59.300 --> 00:51:01.017
the bat more than the
social mobility questions

00:51:01.017 --> 00:51:05.580
is you could say, well,
when Evo came to power,

00:51:05.580 --> 00:51:07.363
maybe there's patronage.

00:51:09.218 --> 00:51:11.460
There's patronage for
indigenous people or something.

00:51:11.460 --> 00:51:12.960
So it could be that,

00:51:12.960 --> 00:51:14.800
it's easier to get a job in the government

00:51:14.800 --> 00:51:18.480
if you're an indigenous
person, so that could be...

00:51:18.480 --> 00:51:22.140
I guess you could say that
could potentially be driving

00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:24.250
these results on income

00:51:24.250 --> 00:51:27.240
so maybe that's a
mechanism you had in mind.

00:51:27.240 --> 00:51:31.870
So that's not the
interpretation that we like.

00:51:31.870 --> 00:51:34.810
So I think that that's why
the naming results are better,

00:51:34.810 --> 00:51:37.060
but we could dig more on that,

00:51:37.060 --> 00:51:39.360
but off the top of my
head, I can't really say

00:51:41.130 --> 00:51:42.130
too much about that.

00:51:43.300 --> 00:51:47.520
- [Sanjeev] Also the area where
MAS is getting good support,

00:51:47.520 --> 00:51:50.890
is also an area that overlaps
with the lithium production.

00:51:50.890 --> 00:51:55.240
And that is a big issue in
Bolivia, in the Southwest area.

00:52:00.570 --> 00:52:04.130
- Yeah, I don't know much
about the organization

00:52:04.130 --> 00:52:07.990
of these mining economies
or how that money

00:52:07.990 --> 00:52:09.100
is redistributed.

00:52:09.100 --> 00:52:10.850
I don't know if that's
very capital intensive,

00:52:10.850 --> 00:52:13.900
what's creating employment
or the rents accrued

00:52:13.900 --> 00:52:16.623
to the state and the
state is redistributing.

00:52:17.630 --> 00:52:19.343
We have these time effects here.

00:52:22.090 --> 00:52:25.763
This is a differential
effect, the indigenous people.

00:52:28.640 --> 00:52:30.740
I don't think a boom, in Bolivia,

00:52:30.740 --> 00:52:34.210
as a consequence of say,
mineral natural resource prices

00:52:34.210 --> 00:52:37.493
can really be driving these
results, I guess I would say.

00:52:38.430 --> 00:52:40.307
- [Sanjeev] Okay, thank you.

00:52:40.307 --> 00:52:43.373
- Jim, I have a question if I may.

00:52:45.750 --> 00:52:47.937
So the paper is really interesting.

00:52:47.937 --> 00:52:49.030
And as it's interesting,

00:52:49.030 --> 00:52:54.030
all these issues about how
societies in Latin America

00:52:56.220 --> 00:52:58.680
have been shaping up and how...

00:52:58.680 --> 00:53:02.220
Where the excess inequality
or the large inequality

00:53:02.220 --> 00:53:04.600
that Latin America come from.

00:53:04.600 --> 00:53:09.300
I really liked all the nuances
that you pointed out too,

00:53:09.300 --> 00:53:12.583
and how the colonial history
and evolution, all that.

00:53:14.750 --> 00:53:19.170
It's also a very important
moment to look at the stakes.

00:53:19.170 --> 00:53:21.670
If you look at what was
happening in Latin America

00:53:23.084 --> 00:53:27.420
before the pandemic in Chile and Colombia,

00:53:27.420 --> 00:53:29.510
with the social unrest,

00:53:29.510 --> 00:53:34.510
that many link to the
absence of community power,

00:53:36.520 --> 00:53:39.500
community organizations where

00:53:39.500 --> 00:53:41.653
citizens could feel represented.

00:53:43.290 --> 00:53:46.130
So all that is nice, it's interesting.

00:53:46.130 --> 00:53:51.010
What I'm left a little bit hesitant

00:53:52.070 --> 00:53:56.100
on embracing your conclusions is fine,

00:53:56.100 --> 00:53:57.610
this is what happened in Bolivia,

00:53:57.610 --> 00:54:02.610
where the indigenous
background is very strong

00:54:03.530 --> 00:54:04.480
and very prevalent.

00:54:07.620 --> 00:54:11.170
In most of Latin America, you cannot think

00:54:11.170 --> 00:54:14.993
of something similar happening.

00:54:16.160 --> 00:54:18.567
One way to interpret what you're saying,

00:54:18.567 --> 00:54:21.240
and I particularly liked
the regressions where,

00:54:21.240 --> 00:54:25.510
the results where the coca
suitability is important,

00:54:25.510 --> 00:54:26.880
but it's not the story.

00:54:26.880 --> 00:54:31.723
The very story is to be inside
or outside that structure,

00:54:32.600 --> 00:54:37.020
they argue but, can you
think of other situations

00:54:37.020 --> 00:54:41.150
in Latin America where
this community level

00:54:41.150 --> 00:54:44.200
empowerment could take place?

00:54:44.200 --> 00:54:46.650
Because I have a very hard time too.

00:54:46.650 --> 00:54:48.400
At least for the countries
that I know best,

00:54:48.400 --> 00:54:53.113
which are, Chile, Columbia,
and to an extent, Mexico,

00:54:53.113 --> 00:54:55.963
(indistinct) in Mexico that
might be something like this.

00:54:57.300 --> 00:54:59.082
- I think that's right.

00:54:59.082 --> 00:55:01.520
I think you're right, in some sense.

00:55:01.520 --> 00:55:06.520
I would say the reason that
this took this very interesting

00:55:06.640 --> 00:55:10.620
and much more inclusive form
is because you have this very

00:55:10.620 --> 00:55:12.833
cohesive indigenous society.

00:55:14.040 --> 00:55:16.690
And that's not true in rural Columbia.

00:55:16.690 --> 00:55:19.750
It's true in little pockets
of Narino or whatever it is

00:55:19.750 --> 00:55:21.600
or Putumayo, but generally speaking,

00:55:21.600 --> 00:55:24.760
it's not true in rural Columbia.

00:55:24.760 --> 00:55:26.920
But I guess I would say,

00:55:26.920 --> 00:55:30.380
I think the broader point
that this literature

00:55:30.380 --> 00:55:32.900
emphasize the hegemonic control of elites

00:55:32.900 --> 00:55:35.120
and there's actually all these spaces for

00:55:35.120 --> 00:55:38.400
alternative political projects to emerge

00:55:38.400 --> 00:55:41.130
and flourish, I think that's true.

00:55:41.130 --> 00:55:41.963
If you think about Peronism,

00:55:41.963 --> 00:55:44.740
thinking about the
history of the Peronism,

00:55:44.740 --> 00:55:46.383
that was exactly that.

00:55:48.586 --> 00:55:51.220
The Descamisados, who
were the Descamisados?

00:55:51.220 --> 00:55:53.770
They were people from the interior.

00:55:53.770 --> 00:55:56.580
They were people from the
interior who mobilized,

00:55:56.580 --> 00:55:58.730
who went to support...

00:55:58.730 --> 00:55:59.640
Again, you might think, well,

00:55:59.640 --> 00:56:02.370
that turned out to be a disaster,

00:56:02.370 --> 00:56:07.210
but I don't have a theorem about
when political mobilization

00:56:07.210 --> 00:56:09.450
turns into something.

00:56:09.450 --> 00:56:11.190
That may well be very conditional

00:56:11.190 --> 00:56:14.660
on the nature of these
traditional Bolivian institutions.

00:56:14.660 --> 00:56:15.693
I agree with that.

00:56:17.024 --> 00:56:19.430
- What your story then
could be read is to say,

00:56:19.430 --> 00:56:22.630
the situation in Latin
America is characterized

00:56:22.630 --> 00:56:27.630
by these big holes that
can be filled by something.

00:56:31.450 --> 00:56:32.990
- That's the story in the United States.

00:56:32.990 --> 00:56:36.180
Isn't Jackson Turner's
thesis about the frontier,

00:56:36.180 --> 00:56:39.253
all about how, it's the
frontier that creates,

00:56:40.527 --> 00:56:42.800
the cake creates the society.

00:56:42.800 --> 00:56:45.530
It was a U.S frontier
with frontier expansion

00:56:45.530 --> 00:56:48.710
and social mobility and
democracy and whatever it was

00:56:48.710 --> 00:56:51.270
that actually created
the U.S, not the core.

00:56:51.270 --> 00:56:54.640
So I think, trying to
think more about the...

00:56:54.640 --> 00:56:57.750
I agree with you, it's specific to the...

00:56:57.750 --> 00:57:01.210
Maybe you can think about this
in Peru or a few other places

00:57:01.210 --> 00:57:04.840
or Guatemala or whatever, but I agree.

00:57:04.840 --> 00:57:07.240
Like many of these mechanisms
are gonna be different

00:57:07.240 --> 00:57:09.210
in different contexts and rural Columbia

00:57:09.210 --> 00:57:13.700
is very different from rural Bolivia.

00:57:13.700 --> 00:57:18.700
But I think the bigger
image I try to project

00:57:18.920 --> 00:57:22.590
at the start, I find interesting,

00:57:22.590 --> 00:57:26.880
but whether you seek validity
of any of these things,

00:57:26.880 --> 00:57:30.420
no, it's very conditional
on these institutions

00:57:30.420 --> 00:57:34.750
but I think, that's why I'm
not an economist anymore.

00:57:38.764 --> 00:57:42.450
- Can I ask a question about
the global mobilization

00:57:42.450 --> 00:57:43.630
of indigenous people?

00:57:43.630 --> 00:57:47.630
I believe there's 47
indigenous peoples in the land.

00:57:47.630 --> 00:57:51.913
I noticed last week at the UN Food Summit,

00:57:54.575 --> 00:57:56.080
that the indigenous peoples

00:57:56.080 --> 00:58:00.690
were part of the lobby for zero hunger.

00:58:00.690 --> 00:58:02.543
And my other question is this,

00:58:04.130 --> 00:58:06.980
within the Catholic
church, there was a Pope,

00:58:06.980 --> 00:58:11.097
John Paul II, as a saint, who
told United nations in 1995

00:58:14.500 --> 00:58:16.573
in New York at the general assembly,

00:58:19.436 --> 00:58:22.150
in this century, we need to have

00:58:22.150 --> 00:58:25.973
a universal right for nations.

00:58:26.970 --> 00:58:29.950
Do you take this whole
global indigenous movement

00:58:31.000 --> 00:58:35.407
has a place in the United
Nations under the banner

00:58:35.407 --> 00:58:38.300
of universal right of nations?

00:58:38.300 --> 00:58:41.590
Can they be considered
nations without sovereignty?

00:58:41.590 --> 00:58:42.423
That's my point.

00:58:44.500 --> 00:58:45.333
- Oh gosh.

00:58:48.320 --> 00:58:51.083
I don't think my findings speak
to any of those questions.

00:58:51.083 --> 00:58:52.840
They're important questions.

00:58:52.840 --> 00:58:55.340
I'm not sure I have anything terribly

00:58:55.340 --> 00:58:57.270
sophisticated to say about them.

00:58:57.270 --> 00:58:59.363
Sometimes during the colonial period,

00:59:00.510 --> 00:59:02.970
this Republica De Indios was...

00:59:02.970 --> 00:59:06.310
It did have a form of
sovereignty or independence.

00:59:06.310 --> 00:59:08.930
The way the colonial state operated,

00:59:08.930 --> 00:59:13.650
it granted enormous autonomy
to these communities

00:59:13.650 --> 00:59:18.527
to self-organize and that's
true all over the world.

00:59:23.155 --> 00:59:26.920
How you recognize that
today in the modern world,

00:59:26.920 --> 00:59:27.753
I don't know.

00:59:27.753 --> 00:59:29.790
Obviously that's happening a lot,

00:59:29.790 --> 00:59:33.150
like there's all of these
people, at least in Bolivia,

00:59:33.150 --> 00:59:36.530
and in Chile now, there's a
very interesting discussion

00:59:36.530 --> 00:59:39.150
in this constitutional assembly about

00:59:39.150 --> 00:59:41.910
indigenous people's
rights and in Colombia,

00:59:41.910 --> 00:59:45.600
after the 1991 constitution,
indigenous people

00:59:45.600 --> 00:59:50.600
got rights to their land, they
got mandated representation

00:59:50.610 --> 00:59:52.420
in national political institutions.

00:59:52.420 --> 00:59:55.010
They were recognized for the first time,

00:59:55.010 --> 00:59:58.770
but that's not on the same
scale as it is in Bolivia

00:59:58.770 --> 01:00:01.071
because indigenous people in Columbia,

01:00:01.071 --> 01:00:03.280
maybe 5% of the population.

01:00:03.280 --> 01:00:08.280
So I don't know, I'm not
sure I'm enough of a scholar

01:00:08.590 --> 01:00:10.440
to be able to answer those questions.

01:00:14.350 --> 01:00:16.830
I think you see indigenous
people everywhere,

01:00:16.830 --> 01:00:19.310
gaining much more
recognition in Australia.

01:00:19.310 --> 01:00:21.010
Look at what's happening in Canada.

01:00:21.010 --> 01:00:23.110
In New Zealand, just this week,

01:00:23.110 --> 01:00:26.130
the Maori people are petitioning to change

01:00:26.130 --> 01:00:29.030
the name of the country
back to its original name

01:00:29.030 --> 01:00:30.960
away from New Zealand.

01:00:30.960 --> 01:00:34.680
So I think you see that
empowerment of indigenous people

01:00:34.680 --> 01:00:36.510
everywhere and that cultural

01:00:36.510 --> 01:00:38.473
flourishing of indigenous people.

01:00:40.540 --> 01:00:41.373
I think what you're suggesting is

01:00:41.373 --> 01:00:43.130
there's a much bigger picture here,

01:00:43.130 --> 01:00:45.960
which this research that
I'm doing is sitting in

01:00:45.960 --> 01:00:50.960
this much bigger picture and
perhaps that helps explain,

01:00:51.150 --> 01:00:54.870
why it was that the MAS were able to grow

01:00:54.870 --> 01:00:57.350
and become empowered like this because,

01:00:57.350 --> 01:00:59.450
it's very complicated because they also...

01:01:01.250 --> 01:01:03.850
Morales is vice-president for example.

01:01:03.850 --> 01:01:07.150
He was a Marxist
sociologists so they also,

01:01:07.150 --> 01:01:10.250
they tapped into this anti neo-liberal,

01:01:10.250 --> 01:01:14.080
anti-Washington consensus
narrative as well.

01:01:14.080 --> 01:01:18.230
But it's an interesting question to ask,

01:01:18.230 --> 01:01:21.030
and I'd have to go and look
at maybe at the qualitative

01:01:21.030 --> 01:01:25.340
sources on this, is to what
extent were they able to

01:01:25.340 --> 01:01:28.660
tap into these broader narratives
of indigenous empowerment

01:01:28.660 --> 01:01:32.290
in a way that helped them
to grow and flourish?

01:01:32.290 --> 01:01:33.760
I'm not sure I know the answer to that

01:01:33.760 --> 01:01:34.690
off the top of my head

01:01:34.690 --> 01:01:37.120
- Thank you for an interesting answer.

